# An out-of-range axis names the axis the caller wrote, on both implementations.
#
# The two used to disagree here, and only here: the Go kernel reported the axis
# AFTER adding the rank, so `sum(m, -7)` on a rank-2 tensor said "axis -5", a
# number that appears nowhere in the program and that a reader has to reverse
# the adjustment to recognise. src/tensor.tw always reported the original. The
# comment above bi_reduce in src/eval.tw asserted the Go behaviour and was wrong
# about the file it sat in.
#
# The axis is computed rather than written as a literal on purpose: a constant
# axis is refused by the checker before either evaluator sees it, so a fixture
# with `sum(m, -7)` in it would be testing the checker instead.
#
# This one runs at the top level rather than inside a `main`, unlike its
# neighbours: those are systems-mode programs, and a numeric-mode `main` is not
# called by either driver, so a fixture shaped that way prints nothing on both
# sides and agrees about nothing.
fn axis_from(k) = 0 - k

let m = zeros(2, 3)
# A negative axis in range still counts from the end, and still works.
print(sum(m, axis_from(1)))
print(sum(m, axis_from(7)))
